<h2>The duty belongs to both parents</h2>
<p>Indian law has never made child maintenance the father&rsquo;s burden alone. Section 20 of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956 obliges a Hindu — expressly, &ldquo;male or female&rdquo; — to maintain legitimate and illegitimate minor children. Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, like Section 125 CrPC before it, speaks of any person having sufficient means who neglects or refuses to maintain his or her minor child unable to maintain itself. Section 26 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 empowers the court seized of a matrimonial case to pass interim and final orders for the custody, maintenance and education of the children. The question in a shared-custody case is therefore never <em>whether</em> both parents must contribute — it is <em>how</em> the contribution is organised when the child lives in two homes.</p>

<h2>Why fifty-fifty time does not mean zero maintenance</h2>
<p>A child&rsquo;s costs do not divide neatly by nights. Some costs follow the calendar — food, utilities, outings. Others are fixed and continuous regardless of where the child sleeps: school fees, uniforms and books, coaching, medical insurance, devices. And some are embedded in each household — a child needs a room, clothing and essentials in both homes, which makes shared custody more expensive in aggregate, not less. The governing principle remains that the child should enjoy a standard of living consistent with both parents&rsquo; means. Where one parent&rsquo;s income is a multiple of the other&rsquo;s, equal time-sharing without any transfer simply produces two unequal childhoods.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Cost type</th><th>Sensible treatment in shared custody</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Fixed institutional costs (school, transport, insurance, coaching)</td><td>Paid directly to the institution in defined shares proportionate to income; recorded in the order.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Calendar-following costs (food, daily travel, recreation)</td><td>Borne by each parent during their custodial time.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Duplicated household costs (room, clothing, essentials in each home)</td><td>Each parent bears their own; relevant when fixing any balancing transfer.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Income disparity between homes</td><td>Addressed by a periodic balancing payment from the higher-earning to the lower-earning parent.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Structuring the order</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Disclose incomes first.</strong> Both parents&rsquo; income and expenditure go on affidavit; the child&rsquo;s budget — fees, classes, medical needs — is itemised separately from each parent&rsquo;s own expenses.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Split the fixed costs by ratio.</strong> Agree or seek a defined ratio for invoice-based costs, payable directly, so neither household handles the other&rsquo;s money.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Fix the balancing figure.</strong> If incomes diverge, quantify a monthly transfer that evens out the child&rsquo;s standard of living across both homes.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Build in review.</strong> School transitions, board years and changes in the time-sharing pattern are predictable variation points; provide for them, or apply under Section 146 BNSS or to the matrimonial court when they arrive.</div>
</div>

<h2>Common friction points</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Informal set-offs are the classic trap: a parent who unilaterally deducts &ldquo;expenses incurred&rdquo; from an ordered maintenance amount risks execution proceedings. Set-offs belong in the order, not in self-help.</p>
  <p>A parent cannot buy out of time-sharing with money, or out of money with time-sharing — custody and maintenance serve different interests of the child, and courts decide each on its own footing.</p>
  <p>Where a DV Act case is also pending, monetary relief for the child under Section 20(1)(d) must be coordinated with any Section 144 BNSS or Section 26 HMA order, so that the arrangement is adjusted rather than duplicated.</p>
  <p>Keep records: fee receipts, insurance premiums and transfers made for the child are the first exhibits in any later variation or enforcement application.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer sets out the general statutory framework; it is not legal advice. Shared-custody maintenance structures are highly fact-specific, and the right design depends on the incomes, the time-sharing pattern and the child&rsquo;s actual budget.</div>
